  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nMostly clear skies are seen on satellite this morning with \nincreasing mid-level clouds to our west over the Pacific. Models \nshow these clouds moving over the region late this afternoon through \nthe night, bringing a nice sunset for some.  High temperatures will \nbe slightly warmer today than yesterday. NBM chances are moderate to \nhigh in seeing temperatures eclipse the 80 degree mark across inland \nOrange County, the northern Inland Empire and inland valleys of SD \nCounty. Temperatures in these areas cool a few degrees by Tuesday \nand Wednesday, with other regions staying fairly similar from day to \nday over the next few days. As pressure heights subtly lower, this \nwill increase the depth of the marine layer. Low clouds and patchy \nfog may form offshore and near coastal areas as early as Tuesday \nmorning, with higher confidence on this occurring by Tuesday night \ninto Wednesday morning.\n\nThe weather pattern begins to shift by Thursday into next weekend. \nLike yesterday, models continue to show an area of low pressure \nentering the Pacific Northwest around Wednesday into Thursday, where \na large troughing pattern begins to set up across the West Coast. \nThis will bring a deeper marine layer and a cooling trend to the \nregion starting Thursday into next weekend. Increased cloud cover \nwith chances for precipitation may begin as early as late Thursday \nnight, though some model solutions have begun to push back rain \nchances later in time. The broad troughing pattern will bring \ntemperatures back below normal with breezier conditions across the \nmountains and deserts. This pattern may also bring little to no \nrainfall at first, where Thursday night into Friday may be mostly \ndry with only isolated light showers for some.\n\nDuring this time period, model guidance begins to diverge on how the \ndirection/strength of the Pac NW low interacts with the possibility \nof a shortwave with increased subtropical moisture that may poise \nitself somewhere to our south or west over the Pacific.  Details in \nwhere these systems develop and trek will be key in determining how \nmuch precipitation falls across the region. Does the Pac NW low stay \nfurther north and the potential subtropical shortwave stays further \nto the south, giving us minimal rainfall? Do they combine forces, \ngiving us a wetter scenario? Something in between? Whatever occurs, \nconfidence continues to increase for wetter and cooler conditions \nfor the Halloween weekend, so make sure to continue to monitor the \nlatest forecast.",
